Yes, very much the same complaint here. I am the packager for all the official Arch Linux packages. The practice of re-tagging on this repo has cost me quite a bit of time in the past and is a regular source of frustration. I've complained about it in the past too, but it keeps happening.

It would be much appreciated if a different workflow could be setup. If a release goes wrong and you want to get some extra commit it, just tag a new minor version. Or figure out a way to run your release checks privately without pushing the tag to the public repo until you know it is good to go.

@reporter You can usually identify what commit a tarball is from (assuming it is a `git archive` generated one) with `zcat package.tar.gz | git get-tar-commit-id`.

> @reporter You can usually identify what commit a tarball is from (assuming it is a `git archive` generated one) with `zcat package.tar.gz | git get-tar-commit-id`.

@participant1 We don't do “use git archive and add a separate tarball with vendored stuff” for Electron. It's impractical as the bulk of the code is patched Chromium, and the “main” repo lives in an `electron/` subdirectory. We just download everything using gclient and remove the irrelevant libraries and binary blobs afterwards (since we would have to do that anyway) — the git metadata do not survive that process.

<a href="https://<redacted-host>/projects/devel:languages:nodejs/packages/nodejs-electron/files/create_tarball.sh?expand=1">See the script we're using.</a>

> regenerates [this list of sources](https://<redacted-host>/archlinux/packaging/packages/electron30/-/blob/main/PKGBUILD?ref_type=heads#L77) that we can checkout using a sources cache that is available across builds

@participant1 Looking at that list, this is something we (openSUSE) very much do not want to do. Half of these libraries are unused in fact (grep our script for `keeplibs`) and our policy requires us to actually keep a copy of the source, not just provide hashes.

(Also, our diff view on OBS seems not to like multiple versioned tarballs).

I am following this discussion for a while now.

@participant4 @participant3 Is there a specific reason to push the tag *so early* [in the release process][^1]? What is the argument against using the hash throughout the release process and push the tag *just before* the associated release is published?

[^1]: I am not familiar with GitHub releases. Maybe that is a requirement.

@participant4 As for as I know in the last month or so since it was fixed the `releases.json` file and website haven't miss-reported releases. Their biggest fault is that they were regularly missing releases that *were* finalized, not the other way around. At least for the last couple cycles I haven't seen that happen.

On the other hand please don't suggest the GitHub Releases feed as a source of truth because that still does get reset sometimes. Not as often as the tags get yanked, but I have seen it.

----

@participant5 It would require some non trivial re-tooling, but it is certainly possible. Using hashes and then switching to tags would be pretty messy and leave them with artifacts without the right meta data baked in. The simplest way would be to use a non-canonical forked repository on the side to test run builds and maybe save artifacts, but it would be a little convoluted. Another approach would be to have the workflow itself read the *proposed* tag data from somewhere (e.g. a workflow trigger rather than an on push) and have it tag internal to the workflow and have the workflow push the tag when it succeeds. There are other ways too, but it's worth acknowledging that this is a huge beast and the workflow they have is deeply ingrained in the build so fixing this would not be trivial.
